A||||3||GT Bastard||Auto-body experts, please help!||||11
Z||000000||GT Bastard||11-01-1999||11:53 PM||||As many of you know, I got my '87 GT from a repairable lot, and I'm in the process of restoring it.  The majority of the damage is on the front end, and I've alread replaced all damaged body panels.  The only problem is making everything line-up properly.  As it turns out, the pass. side of the car is bent backwards and down about 1/2 or a 1/4 of an inch.  This makes it really had to get the hood and nose to line-up perfectly.  I've taken numberous measurements, and the damage is localized to about the first ft. of the frame on the passenger side.  It really isn't extensive damage, but it's enough to make my life hell.  I've talked to a couple body shops, and they want serious money just to "take a look."  One of them said they want 100 bucks just to removed the ground effects so that they can mount it on the rack.  Is all this really necessary?  The damage isn't that bad at all. Aside from that, the body is perfect, and the car is perfectly aligned too.  Can anybody offer my suggestions?<BR>thanks fellas.....||||reg||11
Z||000001||RZURCTN||11-02-1999||12:31 AM||||What if you removed the necessary panels yourself?  If you're talking about getting the frame tweeked back into place on a rack, then you could do most of the work and have it "rack-ready".  Maybe that will save you a couple of bucks.  I don't know of a way to hide it since all the panels mount to the frame.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||11-02-1999||01:42 AM||||I'd get a book on Cartesian coordinates,and a ball of string.You'll have a better idea of where everything is.<BR>I've fixed a couple cars enough to hang sheet metal,that weren't messed up enough to be un-alignable,with a chain,a large tree,and a sledge hammer/large pieces of wood.Start bending the metal at the edge of the dent or bent portion,not where it's the most bent.But I'm not an expert at laser/rack procedures by any means.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||11-02-1999||09:04 PM||||If your car is only off by 1/4 to 1/2 of an inch, it should be able to be tied down by the rear tow hooks or cradle mounting bolts and pulled forward that amount. I cant say for sure without seeing the car though. ||||reg||1
